00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:12.780
Good.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:13.780
Good.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.780
Frank, great to hear from you.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.110
Uh, great to hear.

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:25.300
So I know you touched on this a few weeks
ago and I just wanted to elaborate on it.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:34.360
You touched on the idea that the Bernie Sanders
free public college tuition plan is a fantastic

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:43.000
one, and that there are calls from some to
also look at the costs around in college,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.200
such as, you know, certain fees, textbook
prices, and room, a room and board an area

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:51.450
rent in the area.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.460
I said this or where, who said this?

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.000
This is something I said.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.000
Yeah.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:01.309
Well, you said basically the costs around
in college I, you kind of touching upon,

00:01:01.309 --> 00:01:02.309
okay, yes, yes.

00:01:02.309 --> 00:01:06.119
I knew we were the only part of what we need
to figure out with college is not just how

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:12.109
to, uh, you know, the, the idea of, of, uh,
making to uh, public universities and colleges

00:01:12.109 --> 00:01:15.439
tuition free, but we also need to deal with
the skyrocketing cost as well.

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:16.679
That's for sure.

00:01:16.679 --> 00:01:17.810
Yes.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:25.789
See My, my main issue with the current mainstream
conversation is that I feel like that's off

00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:34.069
being ignored because my fear if we are to
completely make a public college tuition free,

00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:40.509
and that is, it is that now all of a sudden
means markets will see that while students

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:47.560
have more money and we can still exploit that
by raising textbook prices and you know, making

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:48.649
it so

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:49.780
I don't know about that.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:50.780
Here's why.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.179
I don't think that that's the way it would
go down.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:58.400
Because when you talk about making public
college and university tuition free, um, that's

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.809
only public colleges and universities.

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:06.759
And thus if you're going to start skyrocketing
the cost of textbooks and all of these other

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:13.129
things, that's going to make all of the private
colleges and universities even more unaffordable

00:02:13.129 --> 00:02:14.810
relative to the public ones.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.569
And I just don't think, I'm rarely a person
who says no, the market is going to prevent

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:19.569
that from happening.

00:02:19.569 --> 00:02:21.780
But I just don't think the market would bear
that.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.560
I don't think that that's what would happen
at all.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:33.670
Do you think that the industries that are
for profit or the elements of the industry

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:40.959
that are for profit one still try to figure
out how to exploit loopholes in the system?

00:02:40.959 --> 00:02:42.469
That is always going to be the case?

00:02:42.469 --> 00:02:43.469
Yes, absolutely.

00:02:43.469 --> 00:02:47.519
And that's why the plan has to be designed
thoughtfully and accounting for what the for

00:02:47.519 --> 00:02:49.280
profit companies are going to do.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:50.340
There's no question about that.

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:54.599
I just don't think it's going to happen in
the way that you're describing.

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:58.439
Do you think that, hmm.

00:02:58.439 --> 00:03:04.640
See, this is the one question I'd love to
talk with Bernie Sanders about if I were ever

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.409
to be able to, to discuss this with them.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:12.629
If there was a way to set kind of prices either
where they are or make those affordable, uh,

00:03:12.629 --> 00:03:13.680
as well.

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.500
Well then you're talking about price controls.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:20.400
You're talking about price controls on private
colleges and universities, and that starts

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.489
to get a bit, a little bit dicier for a number
of different reasons.

00:03:24.489 --> 00:03:29.900
I think that the idea would be if you make
public colleges and universities tuition free,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.880
it'll put downward pressure on the private
schools because even if the private school

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.939
is better than the public school, which is
not always the case, by the way.

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:42.920
Um, if it's drastically more expensive accounting
for the free college tuition or tuition paid

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:50.769
for through taxes, it may force some of those
private schools to reconsider what they are

00:03:50.769 --> 00:03:52.200
charging.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.939
That would be, that would be wonderful.

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:57.189
And I do think that would be a wonderful side
effect of all of this.

00:03:57.189 --> 00:03:58.189
Yeah.

00:03:58.189 --> 00:04:03.969
And yeah, I just do have my anxieties that,
because I'm always pessimistic about a lot

00:04:03.969 --> 00:04:09.819
of industries that, you know, they don't want
to let go of those margins that they're currently

00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:10.819
making.

00:04:10.819 --> 00:04:12.049
They're all about growth, constant growth.

00:04:12.049 --> 00:04:16.959
I see it everywhere I go with factories and
everywhere I've ever worked.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:23.580
It's just this industry having been just a
few years out of college, I know exactly how

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.889
they tried to nickel and dime you at every
opportunity.

00:04:25.889 --> 00:04:30.009
Yeah, you're right to be, say any, any plan
is going to have

00:04:30.009 --> 00:04:34.240
some, uh, unforeseen consequences and some
negative consequences.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.410
But we want to be thinking about these things
more macro economically.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.660
Uh, John from net bank from Niagara, really
appreciate the call.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.670
Hope to hear from you again.

